//Learning points that could help prevent similar errors
/** 
Always define a base case in recursion

Every recursive method needs a clear stopping condition.

Example:

java
if (n == 0 || n == 1) return 1;
Without this, the method keeps calling itself forever.

Ensure progress toward the base case

Each recursive call should move closer to the stopping condition.

For factorial, that means reducing n each time (n - 1).

If you accidentally call with the same value (calculateFactorial(n)), you’ll loop infinitely.

Validate inputs before recursion

Negative numbers or very large inputs can cause runaway recursion.

Add checks like:

java
if (n < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Negative input not allowed");
Broader Best Practices
Prefer iteration for large computations  
Recursion is elegant but can overflow the stack with big inputs. Iterative loops are safer for factorials and similar problems.

Use logging or print statements for debugging  
Printing the input at each recursive step helps you see where the recursion goes wrong.

Think about algorithm efficiency  
Factorial grows fast, so even correct recursion can hit limits. Consider iterative or memoized approaches if performance matters.
Debugging Mindset
When you see repeated lines in a stack trace, it’s a strong signal of infinite recursion.

Focus on the first line pointing to your code — that’s where the recursion starts.

Ask yourself: “Does this method have a way to stop?” and “Am I moving toward that stop?”
 */
