/** 
Error Analysis: StackOverflowError
Error Description:  
Your program kept calling the same method (calculateFactorial) over and over without stopping. In plain language: the computer ran out of memory space because the factorial function never reached a point where it could stop.

Root Cause:  
The recursion in FactorialCalculator.calculateFactorial (line 15) does not have a proper base case or is not reducing the input correctly. As a result, the method keeps calling itself infinitely.

Solution:  
Update your factorial method to include a clear base case and ensure the recursive call reduces the input:

java
public int calculateFactorial(int n) {
    if (n < 0) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Negative numbers not allowed");
    }
    if (n == 0 || n == 1) {
        return 1; // Base case
    }
    return n * calculateFactorial(n - 1); // Recursive step
}
Learning Points:

Always define a base case in recursive functions to stop the recursion.

Ensure each recursive call moves closer to the base case (e.g., n - 1).

Validate inputs before recursion to avoid invalid or runaway cases.

For large inputs, consider an iterative approach instead of recursion to prevent stack overflow.

Use debug prints or logging to trace recursion flow when diagnosing errors.
 */